{"version":3,"kind":"Notebook","sha256":"635361960c17f16bfdf3452f241837e1afd5754e14b82eba4e0d40f736d17b9f","slug":"lectures.b-loops","location":"/lectures/03b-Loops.ipynb","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"03b - Loops: Brownian Motion","content_includes_title":false,"kernelspec":{"name":"python3","display_name":"gekko","language":"python"},"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Clint Guymon","given":"Clint","family":"Guymon"},"name":"Clint Guymon","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"},{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Brigham Young University","given":"Brigham Young","family":"University"},"name":"Brigham Young University","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-1"}],"github":"https://github.com/clint-bg/comptools","numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"source_url":"https://github.com/clint-bg/comptools/blob/main/lectures/03b-Loops.ipynb","edit_url":"https://github.com/clint-bg/comptools/edit/main/lectures/03b-Loops.ipynb","exports":[{"format":"ipynb","filename":"03b-Loops.ipynb","url":"/comptools/_public/03b-Loops-bcea747be1238fb1488bfee60c4f7ead.ipynb"}]},"widgets":{},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Brownian motion is the random motion of particles suspended in a fluid (a liquid or a gas) resulting from their collision with other fast-moving molecules in the fluid. It is named after the botanist Robert Brown, who first observed such motion in 1827.","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"zUrPlaMTLY"}],"key":"oEAxJLPusx"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A random walk is similar to Brownian motion, except that the steps are taken at regular intervals. The random walk is a discrete-time Markov process in which the particle moves at each time step in a random direction with a fixed step length. The step length is usually chosen to be the lattice spacing, so that the particle can only move to an adjacent site in the lattice.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"KuIZcemn7g"}],"key":"YYjCs8Bi3L"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"These processes are important in many fields of science and engineering. For example, the random walk is used to model the diffusion of particles, the spread of epidemics, and the price trajectory of a stock. The random walk is also used in the Monte Carlo method to simulate systems with a large number of degrees of freedom.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"u5CLK2OeQa"}],"key":"n7UDnlYtrZ"}],"key":"TL8fvqd6Bs"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example Statement A: Random Walk","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"ryMwGmgNyO"}],"identifier":"example-statement-a-random-walk","label":"Example Statement A: Random Walk","html_id":"example-statement-a-random-walk","implicit":true,"key":"qHJubHIuBn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suppose you have a one-dimensional grid of 11 points. At each time step, you move one point to the left or right with equal probability. Or in other words, you flip a coin and if you get heads, you move left, and tails you move right. If you land on the zeroeth or tenth location, you’re done. What is the probability if you start at the third (3rd) location you will end up at the tenth location?","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"RoIRluLR1m"}],"key":"xxgphW4Tl3"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Write a program to estimate this probability. Run the program 10000 times and report (1) the average probability and (2) the average number of steps it takes to reach the end. You can also algebraically solve this problem and your answer should match your simulation.","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"hdknSA6HEW"}],"key":"OpQu78o5sy"}],"key":"Gy41jg81gA"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Proficiencies and Potential Modules:","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"hPCHqwZVux"}],"key":"ZxuKJDxEdv"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While loops","position":{"start":{"line":2,"column":1},"end":{"line":2,"column":1}},"key":"n2JhC4zf0o"}],"key":"wYj918aTaO"}],"key":"SMuFM6UsUg"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nested loops (including counting and incrementing)","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"siB9JH0tzA"}],"key":"POJtpGmRiC"}],"key":"MWPospNE6V"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If then statements","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"vGuZo4p0Ix"}],"key":"JvqDeaXKFN"}],"key":"mcoh11jJxd"},{"type":"listItem"